\ A signal handler's error is dropped by the dispatcher, and the drop must be complete: an
\ unrelated error raised later renders only its own chain. Before the shield, the handler's
\ frame survived and rendered as the innermost row of that later error.
\
\ `kill` delivers to this process synchronously, and the `drop` that consumes its result is
\ the safe point where the handler runs. Both sit at this file's top level, which stays
\ interpreted under eager compilation too, so one golden binds both suites.

use "os/signal" ;
use "posix" ;

SIGUSR1 [ drop this-word-does-not-exist ] on-signal

getpid SIGUSR1 unmake-signal kill drop

boom: ( -- ) [ f "real error" real-error: make-error throw ] ;

boom
